      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s original courthouse building, now the Old Main Arts & Heritage Center, is listed on the National Register of Historic Places.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.wvencyclopedia.org/articles/629",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Construction of Summersville Dam began in 1960 and was dedicated by President Lyndon B. Johnson in 1966, creating Summersville Lake for flatwater recreation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Summersville%2C_West_Virginia",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Summersville hosts an annual Potato Festival that celebrates the region’s agricultural heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Summersville%2C_West_Virginia",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Gauley River, just south of Summersville, is famed for challenging whitewater and is impounded by the dam to form Summersville Lake.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Summersville%2C_West_Virginia",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "During the Civil War, both Union and Confederate troops camped in Summersville, and the town was largely burned by Confederate spy Nancy Hart Douglas before being rebuilt by 1884.",
